data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","sequence_number":1739,"item_id":"msg_01f188e2671398a0016b08898eb1c887d1a3b7c89c7a2f9186","output_index":1,"content_index":0,"text":"{\"queries\":[],\"result_json\":\"{\\\"reviews\\\":[{\\\"actor\\\":\\\"wren\\\",\\\"decision\\\":\\\"develop\\\",\\\"ability\\\":null,\\\"reason\\\":\\\"The supplied related-capability search found hand-twist-sectioned-cord, hand-twist-fiber-cord and hand-join-fiber-cords. The first already implements continuous section-by-section twisting but explicitly limits requestedLength to 0.60 m; its current arguments therefore do not cover the requested longer cord. The second is shorter, and joining separate cords is functionally different. Develop the minimal general extension of the existing sectional implementation, removing only the arbitrary requested-length ceiling while retaining physical limits. Do not duplicate its material, sampling, trial or cord mechanics, or infer that about 1.0 m is feasible from the request.\\\",\\\"specification\\\":\\\"Development handoff: implement general hand-twisting of continuous two-ply fiber cord to a physically feasible requested length, parameterized by materials, requestedLength, mode and workId/targetId. Prefer extending the existing sectional operation rather than creating parallel cord physics. Record code reuse from hand-twist-sectioned-cord and hand-twist-fiber-cord and preserve fiber-cord@1. No splicing, joining, strand generation, strength enhancement or retroactive enlargement of existing cords. Existing short-length behavior and saved work remain compatible.\\\\nPrerequisites: individual study of this extension and the sectional method; passing personal source-and-processing sample covering each actual input at the inherited condition tolerance of at most 0.05; owned, reachable, unreserved material; dry footing, sufficient energy, condition at least 0.5 at regrip/twist/release, and the inherited exclusion radius of actor radius plus half the aligned ply length. The request establishes no new mastery or ownership. Wren reports mastery of the sectional method; validate that at execution, not from public availability. Only reed currently has the required strand law. Unsupported materials need their mechanism developed, not fabricated properties.\\\\nMake inputs retain the existing materials array of exact stackId and quantity in 0.1-unit steps, totaling 0.2-1 unit of one fiber and source, plus a finite positive requestedLength in metres. No exact raw material stack or quantity has been selected here; require actual selection after consultation. item-225-wren-2518 is evidence of a passing tested lot from item-73-wren-819, not a grant of available unworked fiber. item-337-wren-3866 remains the existing finished cord and is not an input to manufacture a longer continuous cord.\\\\nRetain the reed law: 1.8 m stems, density 500 kg/m3 and water share 0.9. For requested twisted length L, each continuous ply needs L x 1.155 + 0.02 m of strand length. Divide conserved solid fiber volume between two plies using that length and the existing diameter calculation; do not multiply volume by section count. Refuse before reservation or cost if strands are too short or the theoretical minimum-ply check fails even with all strands intact. Do not replace the 0.60 m ceiling with another arbitrary range: available strand length, volume, 1-12 mm ply diameter, condition and working space determine admissibility. Round-one separation measures intact fiber and sets broken strands aside as real scrap; insufficient intact fiber or an out-of-range measured diameter ends in the inherited measured shortfall with all material accounted for, not a cord. Conservation includes the workpiece, unworked fiber, loose ends and scrap; none may also remain spendable in the source stack. Preserve source, quantity, processing and condition provenance.\\\\nReserve accepted inputs atomically. With N = ceil(L/0.30), use N consecutive sections of at most 0.30 m, each requiring three paid rounds at 1 energy per round: separate-align/regrip, twist and counter-ply. Charge one additional final release-check round at 1 energy, for 3N+1 work rounds and energy if all complete. For example, a feasible L=1.0 m requires four sections and 13 paid work rounds, not the seven-round cost of the old maximum. Reuse the existing first-section measurement and later-section regrip implementation; section boundaries do not create joins or reset strand continuity. Charge and apply the inherited 0.0005 worked-fiber condition loss once per actually worked round. No tools, water, fuel or extra material are supplied. Sample, inspect and trial modes delegate to the existing implementations: sample consumes/retains its actual 0.1-unit test lot in one round at 0.5 energy; inspect takes one round at 0.2 energy; trial takes one round at 0.5 energy and uses the inherited minimum size, force, grip, duration, damage and fragment rules. Do not reimplement them as new discoveries.\\\\nPersist workId, reserved quantities, measured geometry, section index and endpoints, stage, paid progress, condition, scrap and provenance. Continue only during actual worked world rounds. Low energy, absent work, unsafe space, loss of reach or unsuitable footing pauses without charging an unworked stage. Resume through make with workId only, rechecking present conditions without rerolling measurements or duplicating paid progress. Stable counter-plied sections hold their form; never mark an untwisted or unreleased span complete. Preserve the inherited handling of unsecured partial work rather than introducing a free automatic stabilization mechanism. Cancellation is free; cancellation or death releases unworked material exactly once and leaves the physically worked piece and scrap, at the death location when applicable. Paid time, energy and condition loss are not refunded; no duplicate reservation release, finished cord or automatic completion follows interruption. A cost-free preflight refusal changes nothing; a paid measured failure still consumes its actual work.\\\\nOnly a passed release check creates an ordinary owned, untested fiber-cord@1 product, with measured twisted length, overall length including inherited loose ends, ply diameter, condition and section history. Reuse its existing downstream compatibility with hand-tie-cord-knot, hand-join-fiber-cords and binding operations. Do not change transverse-contact@1 or hand-frap-crossing: the tick-367 assessment of item-337-wren-3866 reported a 0.8911 m limiting requirement for that cord geometry, not a guaranteed fit for a new, thinner cord. A new cord needs its own actual assessment. No automatic knot, lashing, frapping, strength rating or trial pass. Finished cord has no new active upkeep process; apply existing storage, condition, trial and binding wear rules, with no duplicate wear. No passive fabrication occurs while paused, studying or awaiting development. Implement guards, conservation, progress, interruption and release rules in code with regression tests for old lengths, multi-section lengths, physical refusal and resume/cancel/death idempotency; do not require repeated model adjudication.\\\",\\\"learning\\\":1,\\\"experienceReview\\\":\\\"The one-round incremental study costs 0.2 energy and advances only when Wren actually studies after implementation; it does not replace individual study of the prerequisite. Development waiting, requests, consultation, registration and mastery grant no pleasure, satisfaction, information reward or world progress. This operation adds no reward-writing mechanism. If an established experience evaluator applies to actual work, verified measurements or informative failure, it must use observable personally applicable events, functional deduplication, its existing bounded caps, decay and cooldown; repeated inspection or renaming is not new information. Familiar legitimate practice may sustain only the existing modest bounded benefit, without compulsory novelty or guaranteed payout. Physical comfort remains capped at 10, nutrition is unchanged, and protected reward accounts, budgets, goals and permissions are untouched.\\\",\\\"visualReview\\\":\\\"Emit observable reservation, measurement, section-work, pause, resume, shortfall, cancellation, release and trial events only when those events actually occur. Reuse cord visual metadata and add reusable section geometry: workId, source/target identifiers, stage, status, section index, measured and requested lengths, twisted extent, section endpoints, ply diameter, condition and failure reason where measured. Separate the visible worked span from loose reserved fiber and scrap. Show a complete cord only after successful release; failed or paused work must retain incomplete/shortfall presentation. Unknown dimensions remain unknown until measured. Visual length or an animation is not evidence of strength, trial success or frapping fit, and development waiting displays no fabrication success.\\\"}]}\"}","logprobs":[]}

